## 1. Role, Project Overview and Ground Rules

You are building a real, client-ready website for a real Gold Coast lawn and gardening business. This is paid-studio quality work, not a template exercise. The finished site is vanilla HTML, CSS and JS, runs from a double-click on `index.html`, and drops onto any static host.

**Working directory rule (non-negotiable):** Work entirely inside the root folder this Claude Code session is already running in. Do not create a new project folder inside Downloads, OneDrive, Desktop, or anywhere else. This session's current folder IS the project root. All files, images, and assets for Maik Gardens live inside it, and nothing gets written outside it unless you're explicitly told otherwise mid-session.

### Repo and deployment rules (do this check first)

1. Check whether the project root is a git repo connected to GitHub (`git rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree` and `git remote -v`).
2. **If it IS a GitHub repo:** work directly on `main`. Commit and **push everything to `main` every time, always**. No feature branches, no pull requests, no side branches. **No preview URLs of any kind** (no Vercel/Netlify/Cloudflare preview deploys, no branch deploys, no "preview link" in your replies). Commit in small logical chunks with clear messages and push after each one so `main` is never behind the working folder.
3. **If it is NOT a GitHub repo:** disregard all of the git instructions above and just create and update the website files in the project root. Do not run `git init`, do not create a remote, do not suggest one.
4. Either way, never ask the client or the user for a staging link. Review happens on the real files.

### Absolute copy rule: NO EM DASHES

**Strictly no em dashes (the long dash character) anywhere on the website.** That covers visible copy, headings, button labels, alt text, `<title>`, meta descriptions, Open Graph tags, JSON-LD, JS strings, HTML comments, CSS comments, and the filenames. Normal hyphens (`-`) are fine. Use a comma, a full stop, a colon or a plain hyphen with spaces instead. Section 11 has a grep check you must run before you call this finished.

### The real angle, spelled out

Maik Gardens is a Gold Coast lawn and gardening service with a 5.0 star Google rating across 101 reviews. No origin story, founder name or founding year was found publicly (the one directory that lists a founding year uses obvious boilerplate copy, so ignore it). So the site does NOT lean on a made-up heritage. It leans on the thing the reviews actually say, over and over:

- **Efficiency** is the single most mentioned theme in the reviews (19 reviews).
- **Attention to detail** is second (16 reviews).
- Then garden transformations, tidy cleanups, a polite team, fair pricing.

The site's job is simple: show fast, clean, careful work, back it with the real review numbers, and make it one tap to call. Do not write "family owned", "passionate", "years of experience" or any history claim. None of it is confirmed. If the client supplies a real story later, it slots into the About strip on the home page (see section 7).

