## 5. Design Direction

### Clichés to avoid

- No warm-cream background with a terracotta/clay accent and a big serif headline.
- No near-black background with a single neon-green or acid accent used everywhere.
- No hairline-rule "broadsheet" layout with dense newspaper columns.
- No centered hero with a gradient-blob background and a generic stock photo of unrelated smiling people.
- No numbered "01 / 02 / 03" feature cards unless there's a real sequence being described.
- No emoji in copy or UI, no generic icon-pack icons standing in for real photography.
- No filler marketing copy: "world-class," "premier," "unlock your potential," "passionate about delivering excellence." Every claim needs to be something you can back up from the snapshot in section 2.
- No leaf-and-sprout clipart, no cartoon mower, no "eco-friendly green gradient" nonsense.

### Palette (sample from the flyer and logo, these are starting values)

Define as CSS custom properties in `:root`. Re-sample the actual hex values from the logo file and replace these if they differ.

| Token | Starting hex | Why it earns its place |
|---|---|---|
| `--forest` | `#2F3A12` | The deep olive-forest panel colour on the flyer. Main dark surface. |
| `--moss` | `#3F4D1C` | The slightly lighter green band on the flyer. Secondary dark surface and hover states. |
| `--marigold` | `#E9A23B` | The orange-gold type and fork-and-shovel colour in the logo. Used sparingly: headlines on dark, buttons, the odd rule. |
| `--soil` | `#2A221D` | The dark soil disc of the badge. Footer and the phone bar. |
| `--turf` | `#F1F4E8` | A cool, grass-tinted off-white for light sections. Deliberately not cream. |
| `--ink` | `#1B1F10` | Body text on light surfaces. |

Marigold on `--turf` fails contrast for body text, so marigold is for large type on dark surfaces and for button fills with `--ink` text. Check WCAG AA on every pairing you actually use.

### Typography

- **Display:** a bold condensed sans in the same family as the flyer headline (it reads like Bebas Neue / Fjalla One / Oswald territory). Pick **Oswald** or **Fjalla One** unless you find a closer match. All caps for headings, tight but readable tracking.
- **Body:** **Barlow** or **Source Sans 3**, 17 to 18px, generous line height.
- Why: the flyer is a trade-service poster. Heavy condensed caps say "turn up, do the job", and a plain sturdy body face keeps it readable on a phone in a driveway.

### Signature element (spend the boldness here, keep everything else disciplined)

**A torn-edge split hero lifted straight from the flyer.** Left or top: a `--forest` panel with the headline in marigold condensed caps. Right or bottom: a real lawn photo that bleeds in through a ripped, irregular edge (CSS `clip-path: polygon(...)` with an uneven torn line, no image file needed). The round badge logo overlaps the seam, slightly rotated (about -4deg), with a soft shadow, exactly like it sits on the flyer. The torn edge returns once more as the divider into the footer, and nowhere else. No other decorative effects.

### Motion

Sparse and deliberate. A gentle scroll reveal on section headings and cards, and a hover/focus lift on buttons. **Respect `prefers-reduced-motion` completely:** when set, no reveal, no transform, no animation at all, not just reduced. No parallax, no floating shapes, no animation scattered everywhere.

### Quality floor (always)

Fully responsive down to 360px wide. Visible keyboard focus state on every interactive element (never `outline: none` without a replacement). Real descriptive alt text on every image. Semantic HTML5 (`header`, `nav`, `main`, `section`, `footer`), exactly one `h1` per page, correct heading order.

### Mobile-first note

Most visitors will arrive on a phone, from Google, wanting a mower guy. Design phone-first. Add a **sticky bottom call bar on mobile** (hidden at 900px and up): a full-width `--soil` bar with a marigold "Call 0406 851 039" button. It must not cover page content (pad the bottom of the body) or the footer links.

